  "series": "cup",
  "notes": [
    "Alan Kulwicki led the most laps in the race by a scant one lap over winner Bill Elliott to tie Elliott in points for the race. Because he entered the race 10 points ahead of Elliott, he won the 1992 Winston Cup championship by 10 points. Kulwicki would never again lead the points standings.",
    "Mathematically, Davey Allison needed to finish in sixth place or better to guarantee he would win the championship; he was collected in an accident with Ernie Irvan late in the race. Allison's team repaired his damaged car in the garage area, and he finished the race just above the minimum allowed speed.",
    "Six drivers entered the Hooters 500 with a mathematical chance to win the championship - Allison, Kulwicki, Elliott, Harry Gant, Kyle Petty, and Mark Martin. Gant was not a factor to finish highly in the race, Petty was running in the top 10 before a late race mechanical issue, and Martin was a contender for the win until he suffered a blown engine.",
    "Richard Petty finished 35th in his final career start.",
    "Jeff Gordon finished 31st in his first career start."
  ],
